"""Topics and explanations for CPRITE 2023 Q101–150."""

META: dict[int, tuple[str, str]] = {
    101: ("Psychopathology",
         "Difficulty waking for school plus weekend sleep-ins is delayed sleep-phase type circadian rhythm sleep disorder: sleep is consolidated but the clock runs late. Narcolepsy is sleep attacks, cataplexy, or sleep-onset REM, not a shifted-but-intact night. Restless legs and obstructive apnea fragment sleep; hypersomnolence disorder is excess sleep without a phase delay. Teaching point: Hard mornings, weekend catch-up, otherwise normal sleep = delayed sleep-phase circadian disorder."),
    102: ("Development",
         "From a sociobiologic and ethologic view, reproduction is the behavior that actually perpetuates a species. Aggression, competition, and resource-holding potential are instruments that can serve mating and offspring care; they are not the fitness endpoint. Intelligence is neither necessary nor sufficient for species survival. Teaching point: Sociobiology's survival currency is reproduction, not fighting or IQ."),
    103: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "A staff photo of an identified patient on social media is an unauthorized disclosure. The first step is to have the staff member take the post down, stopping ongoing harm. Telling the family, a licensing report, or reassigning the staff may follow, but they do not unpublish the picture. This is not an acceptable patient interaction. Teaching point: Unauthorized patient photo online → remove the post first."),
    104: ("Development",
         "Thomas and Chess's activity dimension is the child's baseline motor energy. Channeling a high-activity five-year-old into soccer and rule-bound physical games matches that trait. Intensity is how strong reactions are; rhythmicity is schedule regularity; adaptability is ease of change; distractibility is how easily attention is pulled. Teaching point: High motor energy = activity, not intensity."),
    105: ("Neuroscience",
         "The dentate gyrus of the hippocampus is the established site of postnatal CNS neurogenesis in humans. Olfactory-bulb neurogenesis is clear in rodents but is not the human finding this item keys. Thalamus, basal ganglia, and orbitofrontal cortex are not postnatal neurogenic niches. Teaching point: Human postnatal neurogenesis → hippocampus, not olfactory bulb."),
    106: ("Assessment",
         "Falls, Gowers-like difficulty rising, trips, and muscle pain are a myopathy (classically Duchenne), not conversion. Electromyography (with CK/NCS as indicated) shows a myopathic pattern; conversion has a normal study. Head CT, MRI, EEG, and PET do not distinguish muscle disease from a functional presentation. Teaching point: Weakness that looks conversion-like but is proximal and painful → EMG, not another brain scan."),
    107: ("Neuroscience",
         "Adolescent emotion dysregulation is explained by delayed prefrontal cortical maturation: limbic threat/reward circuits come online before the PFC can brake them. Limbic subcortical systems are not delayed; they mature earlier. Myelination and synaptic pruning are underway but do not themselves explain the regulatory lag. Nucleus accumbens hyper-reactivity speaks more to reward-seeking than to control failure. Teaching point: Teen affect storms = PFC still catching up, not a late amygdala."),
    108: ("Psychopathology",
         "Two years of hallucinations, negative symptoms (isolation, flat affect), academic collapse, and odd laughing in a 12-year-old with a negative medical workup is early-onset schizophrenia. Bipolar needs a mood episode, not a chronic deteriorating psychosis. Autism is early social-communication delay, not new psychotic symptoms at school age. RAD is attachment; DMDD is chronic irritability without psychosis. Teaching point: Childhood-onset progressive psychosis plus negatives = early-onset schizophrenia."),
    109: ("Assessment",
         "Narcolepsy on sleep testing shows sleep-onset REM (SOREMPs), usually on MSLT after overnight PSG. Long N2, low total sleep time, high efficiency, and extra slow-wave sleep are not the signature. Teaching point: Narcolepsy's PSG/MSLT fingerprint = REM as you fall asleep."),
    110: ("Development",
         "Hiding in plain sight because she does not want to be seen is Piagetian egocentrism: the preoperational child assumes others share her viewpoint. Conservation is concrete-operational quantity invariance. Accommodation is Piaget's schema change. Object permanence is sensorimotor. Deductive reasoning is formal operational. Teaching point: If I can't see you, you can't see me = egocentrism."),
    111: ("Psychopathology",
         "ADHD is the most common psychiatric comorbidity in fetal alcohol spectrum disorder (often a majority of clinic samples). ODD, conduct, IED, and anxiety occur but at lower rates. Teaching point: FASD → look for ADHD first."),
    112: ("Systems & Prevention",
         "CASSP's service-delivery principles include coordinated, community-based, family-focused care, with case management as the mechanism that holds agencies together. Discharging for nonadherence, easy residential access, handing out samples, and generic abuse-reporting rules are not CASSP guiding principles (residential care also violates least-restrictive-setting). Teaching point: CASSP runs on case management and coordination, not on residential-first care."),
    113: ("Neuroscience",
         "Recurrent dizziness, vertigo, occipital headache, and transient hemiparesis that resolve within 24 hours with a normal exam is basilar artery migraine (migraine with brainstem aura). Tension-type headache has no focal signs. Cyclical vomiting is a GI migraine equivalent without this neurology. Confusional migraine is agitated encephalopathy more than hemiparesis. Conversion is last, after a migraine workup. Teaching point: Occipital headache + vertigo + transient focal signs = basilar migraine."),
    114: ("Research Methods",
         "Power is 1 minus beta: the probability of detecting a real effect, i.e. a true positive. Simply 'rejecting the null' includes false positives (alpha). Avoiding a false positive is 1 minus alpha. Confirming the null is not what frequentist tests do; a 95% CI is precision, not power. Teaching point: Power = chance of a true positive, not 'getting p < 0.05.'"),
    115: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "Malpractice needs duty, dereliction, direct cause, and damages. The psychiatrist warned about gynecomastia, documented consent, and used the medication within a reasonable standard of care, so there is no dereliction. A note does not by itself grant immunity. Absence of 'disability,' a six-month window, and unprovable causation are not why the claim fails. Teaching point: Informed, documented, on-label care meets the standard; paperwork alone does not."),
    116: ("Assessment",
         "Actigraphy plus a sleep diary maps real-world timing and duration over days at home. Polysomnography is one lab night for apnea, parasomnias, or narcolepsy staging. Pulse oximetry, ECG, and EEG answer oxygenation, rhythm, and seizure questions, not the child's typical schedule. Teaching point: Home sleep pattern → actigraphy + diary, not a one-night PSG."),
    117: ("Neuroscience",
         "Hypersomnolence with REM within five minutes is narcolepsy type 1: hypocretin/orexin is low or absent. Leptin, ghrelin, and neuropeptide Y are appetite peptides. Melanin-stimulating hormone is not the wake-stability signal. Teaching point: Sleep attacks plus sleep-onset REM = low orexin."),
    118: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Life-threatening mania (messianic delusions, refusing food and drink, intermittent bradycardia) after dystonic disasters on valproate plus antipsychotics is an ECT indication. Lithium is reasonable in ordinary mania but is too slow and oral-dependent here. Biofeedback, ketamine, and rTMS are not acute treatments for delirious/malignant mania. Teaching point: Starving, psychotic, medication-intolerant mania → ECT."),
    119: ("Psychopathology",
         "Chronic medically unexplained abdominal pain in a 'good kid who never shows stress' is somatic symptom disorder, and the strongest listed risk is an internalizing, emotion-avoidant coping style. Age, male sex, the belly as the site, and being an athlete are weaker or non-risks; the vignette is written around suppressed affect. Teaching point: Somatization risk = how the child copes, not where it hurts."),
    120: ("Neuroscience",
         "Velocardiofacial (22q11.2) syndrome is a microdeletion and is the genetic lesion most repeatedly tied to early-onset psychosis. Methylation is imprinting (Angelman/Prader-Willi). Trinucleotide expansion is Fragile X/Huntington. Polymorphisms and translocations are other mechanisms, not 22q. Teaching point: VCFS / 22q11.2 = deletion, and it raises psychosis risk."),
    121: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Time-out is usually negative punishment, but here it ended the aversive homework, tantrums then increased, so the protocol functioned as negative reinforcement (escape). Extinction is withholding reinforcement. Positive punishment would add an aversive. Positive reinforcement would add a reward. Teaching point: If time-out lets the child escape the demand and the behavior grows, it is negative reinforcement."),
    122: ("Systems & Prevention",
         "Universal prevention reaches the whole population without a risk screen. Community urban planning (housing, parks, density) is a social-determinant intervention at that level. Early intervention for children already at risk is selective. Housing homeless families and FEP psychoeducation are indicated. Punishing parents is not prevention. Teaching point: Urban planning for everyone = universal SDH intervention."),
    123: ("Systems & Prevention",
         "Surveys of the public find that most mental-health information comes from news media and the internet. Insurers, hospital systems, federal agencies, and advocacy groups are real players in care but are not how most Americans first learn the topic. Teaching point: The public's mental-health curriculum is the news and the web."),
    124: ("Consultation & Schools",
         "AACAP consensus for chronic unexplained medical symptoms is concurrent medical workup and psychiatric consultation during the same admission, not sequential 'clear medically, then psych.' Discharge with only an outpatient psych referral, a psych-only admission, or deferring psychiatry while GI works in isolation all miss that timing. Teaching point: Somatic crises get medicine and psychiatry on the same admission."),
    125: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Prescribing the symptom ('argue once a day') is a paradoxical directive from strategic family therapy (Haley/Madanes). Systemic (Milan) uses circular questions and neutrality. Structural rearranges coalitions and boundaries. Psychodynamic family work interprets meaning. Solution-focused looks for exceptions, not symptom prescriptions. Teaching point: Paradoxical 'keep fighting' = strategic."),
    126: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "For acute agitation in a medically stable child with ASD already benefiting from risperidone, give an extra dose of the home antipsychotic that works. Benzodiazepines and diphenhydramine often paradoxically disinhibit in ASD/ID. A new antipsychotic (olanzapine) stacks D2 blockade. Clonidine is slower and weaker for this degree of escalation. Teaching point: ED agitation on a working antipsychotic → extra dose of that med, not a benzo."),
    127: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Fluvoxamine is a CYP1A2 substrate; smoking/nicotine-related 1A2 induction drops levels and can unmask OCD. A paleo diet, an OCP, a sudden 'developmental' metabolic jump, and true SSRI tolerance are not the usual sudden-relapse story at a stable 200 mg. Teaching point: Fluvoxamine relapse after nicotine → think CYP1A2 induction."),
    128: ("Neuroscience",
         "Radial glial cells form the scaffolds that migrating cortical neurons climb during prenatal neurogenesis. Mature astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, macrophages, and non-pyramidal neurons are not that scaffold. Teaching point: Neuronal migration rides radial glia."),
    129: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "After transfer to primary care, the psychiatrist's duty of confidentiality for what was already learned persists. Fidelity, autonomy, beneficence, and nonmaleficence are duties of an ongoing treatment relationship, not leftover obligations a year after discharge. Teaching point: You can close the case; you cannot gossip about it later."),
    130: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "A forensic evaluation starts with the limits of confidentiality (the report goes to the retaining party/court) and the absence of a doctor-patient treatment relationship. Relieving suffering, recording mandates, 'confirming a diagnosis,' and relying only on the youth's self-report are not the required opening warning. Teaching point: Forensic opener = this is not confidential treatment."),
    131: ("Neuroscience",
         "Fatigue, bilateral leg weakness, unilateral vision loss, and contrast-enhancing white-matter lesions in optic tract and periventricular regions in a previously healthy adolescent is multiple sclerosis (optic neuritis plus dissemination). Meningitis is acute and febrile. CNS lymphoma is a mass, often EBV/HIV. ALD is typically boys with posterior leukodystrophy and adrenal failure. SLE can mimic MS but is not the first reading of this scan. Teaching point: Teen optic neuritis + periventricular plaques = MS."),
    132: ("Development",
         "Autistic adolescents and young adults differ from typical peers in identifying less often with birth-assigned gender (higher gender diversity). Interest in a romantic partner and in learning about sexuality is generally similar. Bisexuality and homosexuality rates are not the difference this item keys. Teaching point: ASD youth have more gender-identity variance, not less romantic interest."),
    133: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "Working to keep socioeconomically vulnerable children from being disproportionately antipsychotic-exposed is justice (fair distribution of the benefits and burdens of care). Fidelity is loyalty to a patient. Veracity is truth-telling. Autonomy is the patient's choice. Confidentiality is privacy. Teaching point: Closing a disparity in who gets antipsychotics = justice."),
    134: ("Psychotherapy",
         "A reflective, open-ended question that evokes the patient's own reasons to stop diuretics and regain weight is motivational interviewing (developing discrepancy). Psychoeducation would lecture. Supportive therapy would soothe. DBT would skills-coach the urge. CBT would test a thought record. Teaching point: Open question about their own reasons for change = MI."),
    135: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Very-early-onset schizophrenia with waxy flexibility, echo phenomena, and dangerous refusal of food and fluid that has already failed benzodiazepines and atypicals is catatonia headed to ECT. Lithium, clozapine, and olanzapine are too slow (and neuroleptics can worsen catatonia). rTMS is not the rescue. Teaching point: Benzo-refractory catatonia, even in VEOS, → ECT."),
    136: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "ANC 1300 (mild neutropenia, 1000-1499) without infection: continue clozapine and monitor three times weekly, with hematology input. Weekly monitoring is for normal counts. Interrupting and daily checks are for moderate (500-999) or severe (<500) neutropenia. Teaching point: Clozapine ANC 1000-1499 → continue, CBC 3x/week."),
    137: ("Neuroscience",
         "Subacute psychosis, memory loss, seizure, dysautonomia, and an ovarian lesion in an adolescent girl is anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis (teratoma). CJD is older and rapidly dementing. HSV is febrile and temporal. LEMS is fatigable weakness with small-cell lung cancer. VGKC/LGI1 encephalitis is typically older adults with faciobrachial seizures, not an ovarian tumor. Teaching point: Teen girl + psychosis + seizure + ovarian mass = anti-NMDA."),
    138: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Restlessness, anxiety, and pacing after starting aripiprazole is akathisia. First-line is propranolol (or a dose cut). Benztropine treats dystonia/parkinsonism and is a weak akathisia drug. Valproate treats mania, not restlessness. Raising or switching the antipsychotic usually worsens akathisia. Teaching point: Aripiprazole pacing → propranolol, not benztropine."),
    139: ("Psychopathology",
         "Mild chest pain plus high health anxiety and hours of checking medical information, without panic attacks, feigning, a fixed somatic delusion, or a functional neuro deficit, is illness anxiety disorder. Panic needs recurrent panic attacks. Factitious is conscious production of illness. Conversion is a neurologic symptom. Teaching point: Preoccupation with having a disease, not a dramatic symptom = illness anxiety."),
    140: ("Neuroscience",
         "In the acute-threat model of impulsive aggression, the prefrontal cortex weighs costs and benefits and can brake the response. The amygdala detects threat, periaqueductal gray executes defense, hippocampus supplies context, and caudate is more habit/compulsion than this cost-benefit gate. Teaching point: PFC decides whether the fight is worth it; amygdala just sounds the alarm."),
    141: ("Systems & Prevention",
         "Psychological First Aid: Listen, Protect, and Connect is delivered to all disaster-exposed children without a symptom screen, i.e. universal prevention. Coping Cat treats identified anxiety. MST and Aggression Replacement Training treat established antisocial/aggressive youth (indicated). CBITS is selective/indicated for trauma-exposed students with symptoms. Teaching point: PFA for everyone after a disaster = universal; Coping Cat/MST/CBITS are not."),
    142: ("Psychopathology",
         "SLE plus mutism, food/fluid refusal, odd postures, no diurnal fluctuation, and a lorazepam-induced clearing is catatonia (including a positive lorazepam challenge). Delirium fluctuates. Psychosis does not produce waxy postures as the core. Conversion is not this global motor shutdown. NMS needs a neuroleptic and rigidity/fever, not a benzo rescue. Teaching point: Odd postures + benzo reversal = catatonia, even in lupus."),
    143: ("Assessment",
         "The Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure is the standard copy-and-recall task for visuomotor integration (and visuospatial construction/memory). Rorschach and Roberts are projective. WIAT is achievement. KABC is a cognitive battery, not a visuomotor figure copy. Teaching point: Visuomotor integration test = Rey-Osterrieth."),
    144: ("Assessment",
         "Trauma can produce voices, distorted beliefs, stress-triggered flares, and repetitive play that look psychotic. Disorganized thought and behavior are what pull toward a primary psychotic disorder. Teaching point: Trauma-related 'psychosis' usually lacks disorganization."),
    145: ("Neuroscience",
         "Brief spells of vertigo, ataxia, and nystagmus without loss of consciousness in a preschooler are benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood, a migraine precursor. Tics, chorea, epilepsy, and narcolepsy are not the associated later condition. Teaching point: Toddler spinning/nystagmus spells → later migraine, not epilepsy."),
    146: ("Development",
         "Berry's integration (biculturalism) keeps heritage culture and participates in the host culture: fluent English with American friends and pride in Spanish and pupusas with Salvadoran family. Assimilation would drop the heritage. Separation would reject the American side. Marginalization loses both. Adaptation is not one of the four strategies. Teaching point: Both cultures, on purpose = integration."),
    147: ("Assessment",
         "Sleep attacks, sleep paralysis, and hypnopompic hallucinations are narcolepsy until proven otherwise; after the interview, the essential test is a multiple sleep latency test (following overnight PSG). Actigraphy maps circadian timing. Holter is cardiac. MRI is structural. The maintenance of wakefulness test measures treated alertness, not diagnosis. Teaching point: Narcolepsy workup = MSLT, not MWT."),
    148: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Scolding that makes loud morning play worse is serving as reinforcement (attention). The plan is differential attention: praise quiet play, ignore loud play. It is not functioning as punishment if the behavior is increasing. Omission of reward is extinction. Conditioned/unconditioned stimuli are classical-conditioning terms. Teaching point: If scolding increases the behavior, the scolding is the reinforcer."),
    149: ("Psychopathology",
         "Among the listed childhood diagnoses, ADHD is keyed as carrying the greatest later risk of alcohol use disorder. Conduct disorder and ODD are the usual trap (they do raise externalizing and SUD risk) but are not this item's answer. MDD and GAD contribute less. Teaching point: Childhood ADHD is the keyed predictor of adult AUD here, not conduct disorder."),
    150: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Kohut's self-psychology treats childhood narcissism (grandiose self, idealizing needs) as a normal developmental line, not a diagnosis. Ego is structural theory. Avoidance is a defense. Mentalization is Fonagy. Object constancy is Mahler. Teaching point: Self-psychology: kids are supposed to be narcissistic."),
}
